<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Ashdod</h1><p class="st-meta">1175 BCE – 604 BCE (approx.) · capital Ashdod</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ashdod, a Philistine city of the coastal plain, famed in the Bible for its temple of Dagon and later for revolt against Assyria.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ashdod had been a Canaanite town before Philistine newcomers settled there in the 12th century BCE, making it one of the cities of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/philistine-pentapolis">Philistine Pentapolis</a>. Excavations at Tel Ashdod uncovered Aegean-style pottery and figurines from this period.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its kings ruled the city and a harbour on the coast. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Bible</a> tells of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ark-of-the-covenant">Ark of the Covenant</a> placed in its temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagon</a>. In 712 BCE King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamani">Yamani</a> led a revolt against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> crushed it, setting up a victory stele in the city.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ashdod remained an Assyrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, survived a long Egyptian siege under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-i">Psamtik I</a>, and lost its kings to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a>, who deported them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> c. 604 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ashdod.jpg" alt="Ashdod" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: מקף־עברי · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D7%A4%D7%90%D7%A8%D7%A7_%D7%95%D7%90%D7%92%D7%9D_%D7%94%D7%9E%D7%A8%D7%99%D7%A0%D7%94_%D7%9C%D7%A7%D7%A8%D7%90%D7%AA_%D7%A1%D7%95%D7%A3_%D7%A2%D7%91%D7%95%D7%93%D7%95%D7%AA_%D7%94%D7%91%D7%A0%D7%99%D7%99%D7%94,_%D7%9E%D7%A8%D7%A5_2024.jpg_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1175 BCE – 604 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ashdod</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Azuri, Yamani, Mitinti</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Philistine religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Philistine, Canaanite</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Philistine Ashdod</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-890&amp;focus=ashdod" aria-label="Ashdod on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5573 1020 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ashdod lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5893 1276L6092 1134M5893 1276L6140 1251"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0M6140 1251h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5893 1276h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5893 1276h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-890&amp;focus=ashdod">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

